1
00:00:01,060 --> 00:00:06,063
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking about this practice of contemplation.

2
00:00:06,564 --> 00:00:14,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplating, for example, the danger and sensuality, or various types of contemplation.

3
00:00:15,890 --> 00:00:17,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you describe how to do that?

4
00:00:19,152 --> 00:00:24,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, do you recite some things that you've heard, or what do you do?

5
00:00:25,517 --> 00:00:27,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, so that's like the...

6
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:33,241
[SPEAKER_01]: The contemplation has to start by learning what you need to contemplate.

7
00:00:34,522 --> 00:00:56,210
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can, you know, you can start on your own, but your experience, top to bottom, is already affected with avijja, already affected with ignorance, which means that contemplation will be biased, will have a... like a... will contain contradictions that you're unaware of, and it would be... it would not be necessarily representing the nature of things, how they actually are.

8
00:00:57,190 --> 00:00:58,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order for you to start

9
00:00:59,858 --> 00:01:07,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Undoing all of those issues through right contemplation, you need to really learn what you need to contemplate, what you need to see.

10
00:01:07,666 --> 00:01:09,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why learning is important.

11
00:01:09,973 --> 00:01:11,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning for the sake of meaning.

12
00:01:11,594 --> 00:01:14,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you learn something so to understand what you need to do and see.

13
00:01:15,495 --> 00:01:23,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just learn so you have information to counter somebody else or for the comparison between different aspects of the of the suttas and so on.

14
00:01:23,818 --> 00:01:33,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes people confuse learning with just, as I said, collecting a vast amount of references and information and then when they hear one point, they connect it to another point they heard.

15
00:01:34,622 --> 00:01:36,464
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like, well, that's kind of irrelevant.

16
00:01:36,824 --> 00:01:40,327
[SPEAKER_01]: What's relevant is that when you hear one point, have you developed it?

17
00:01:40,387 --> 00:01:41,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you followed it through?

18
00:01:41,708 --> 00:01:45,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you contemplated it until the meaning sunk in and now you know for yourself?

19
00:01:46,717 --> 00:01:53,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Or does your contemplation stop with just comparison between the suttas and different things that the Buddha said, that you kind of adopted now as the truth?

20
00:01:54,526 --> 00:02:05,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the practice of contemplation then, well, if you learn it from the suttas, one of the first and most obvious things will be that, well, you don't... you can't really contemplate

21
00:02:07,658 --> 00:02:16,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Accurately, the nature of your experience, the nature of your action, the nature of wholesome, the nature of unwholesome, unless you begin to emulate.

22
00:02:19,003 --> 00:02:34,215
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if you're running around wild, like a wild animal, letting your mind go here and there, letting your physical behavior do what it wants, that contemplation will not have a fertile ground.

23
00:02:34,816 --> 00:02:39,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You might be pondering about things theoretically, abstractly,

24
00:02:40,265 --> 00:02:49,308
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want the meaning of that contemplation to start affecting your experience, your behavior, you need to start tailoring that behavior beforehand.

25
00:02:49,808 --> 00:02:51,209
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the virtue comes first.

26
00:02:52,309 --> 00:02:58,991
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what I'm basically saying in simple terms is there is no contemplation unless you have developing virtue and sense restraint as its basis.

27
00:02:59,992 --> 00:03:01,032
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but say you do.

28
00:03:01,492 --> 00:03:07,474
[SPEAKER_01]: You are keeping the precepts, you are celibate, you are guarding of the sense doors, you're watchful of your intentions.

29
00:03:08,415 --> 00:03:09,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you contemplate

30
00:03:10,941 --> 00:03:17,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Just take a sutta, read a sutta, see what it talks about, and then try and understand those things for yourself.

31
00:03:17,665 --> 00:03:27,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Not... Well, initially, to be on the level of making sense out of it, but then also, like, you need to see it for yourself here and now, in this experience.

32
00:03:28,371 --> 00:03:30,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the point of every sutta.

33
00:03:30,473 --> 00:03:34,475
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, technically, if you understand one full sutta, you can be an arahant, any sutta.

34
00:03:35,754 --> 00:03:40,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So, contemplation, then, begins by recognizing the universals from that sutta.

35
00:03:41,038 --> 00:03:42,419
[SPEAKER_01]: What is applicable here and now?

36
00:03:42,639 --> 00:03:45,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why most of the suttas, they always talk about that.

37
00:03:45,741 --> 00:03:48,923
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of craving, the nature of assumptions, the nature of wrong views.

38
00:03:49,764 --> 00:03:52,386
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is pretty much what you need to see here and now.

39
00:03:52,466 --> 00:03:54,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start contemplating by...

40
00:03:55,368 --> 00:03:57,009
[SPEAKER_01]: In this case, you could just, for example, say...

41
00:03:58,170 --> 00:04:01,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself, why am I suffering?

42
00:04:01,372 --> 00:04:04,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, asking right questions is the beginning of successful contemplation.

43
00:04:05,919 --> 00:04:11,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, sorry, keeping the precepts and guarding the sense doors is the beginning of successful contemplation.

44
00:04:11,563 --> 00:04:15,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, asking the right questions can sort of take you in the in the right direction.

45
00:04:15,787 --> 00:04:16,767
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I suffering?

46
00:04:16,867 --> 00:04:17,948
[SPEAKER_01]: That can be a contemplation.

47
00:04:18,949 --> 00:04:21,011
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it can be anything that you get from the Sutta.

48
00:04:21,031 --> 00:04:26,655
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha talks about monks, you know, the eyes are burning, their senses are burning.

49
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:28,957
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, okay, so what did he really mean by that?

50
00:04:28,977 --> 00:04:30,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, everybody can make sense out of it.

51
00:04:31,637 --> 00:04:36,858
[SPEAKER_01]: But, there is, there is a layer of, there is a level of making sense out of it, intellectually.

52
00:04:37,298 --> 00:04:43,700
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, there's a layer of, if you actually understood it, where it's supposed to be understood, you would be free from your senses.

53
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:45,460
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to recognize that.

54
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, that's another contemplation you can do.

55
00:04:48,381 --> 00:04:50,682
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent of the sutta application.

56
00:04:51,442 --> 00:04:56,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, by making that clearer, well, you have a better chance of applying it correctly.

57
00:04:57,293 --> 00:05:00,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, asking questions such as, why am I suffering?

58
00:05:00,156 --> 00:05:01,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I liable to suffering?

59
00:05:02,498 --> 00:05:05,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Could I suffer if nothing is held dear to me?

60
00:05:08,944 --> 00:05:11,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it possible to be equally liable to pain?

61
00:05:12,183 --> 00:05:20,085
[SPEAKER_01]: If I'm sensory-strained, eh... withdrawn from unwholesome things, or if I'm just careless, acting out of desires and proliferating and scratching the itch further.

62
00:05:20,586 --> 00:05:30,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, pretty much, eh... anything that pertains to the nature of experience, nature of desire, nature of craving, freedom from it, can be... can be used for contemplation.

63
00:05:30,369 --> 00:05:31,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is the point.

64
00:05:31,749 --> 00:05:37,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Actively thinking about it, eh... so that the meaning of it actually sinks in.

65
00:05:37,591 --> 00:05:40,191
[SPEAKER_01]: So, next time you won't have to ask that question.

66
00:05:40,231 --> 00:05:41,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you understand.

67
00:05:42,212 --> 00:05:45,595
[SPEAKER_01]: That attachment is the basis for suffering.

68
00:05:45,955 --> 00:05:46,495
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that.

69
00:05:46,735 --> 00:06:00,766
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from now, from then onwards, whenever you recognize an attachment in your experience, you don't need to go through the motions of, oh, well, this is suffering, this is, this is the root of pain, because if you do, means you haven't understood it fully.

70
00:06:01,286 --> 00:06:03,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you haven't contemplated thoroughly enough.

71
00:06:04,098 --> 00:06:15,713
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you did, recognizing attachment will already carry in the implication, this must be undone, without any further questions asked, because you already saw a direct connection between that and pain, and liability to pain.

72
00:06:16,835 --> 00:06:21,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and really that's like the only method that you can say,

73
00:06:22,562 --> 00:06:24,344
[SPEAKER_01]: That can lead you to awakening.

74
00:06:24,524 --> 00:06:35,131
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that diligent contemplation of your motivation, of the nature of experience, not in abstract terms, but in terms of your liabilities, your desires, your attachments, and so on.

75
00:06:35,692 --> 00:06:42,957
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said before, I think, like a long time ago, when I was a layman, you know, you start learning Buddhism from all sorts of sources.

76
00:06:42,977 --> 00:06:46,479
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't necessarily know what's right, what's wrong.

77
00:06:46,599 --> 00:06:51,283
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, I was doing meditation techniques and loads, I don't know for how long as well,

78
00:06:52,723 --> 00:06:55,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the example I always give was like...

79
00:06:55,805 --> 00:06:56,246
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember...

80
00:06:56,926 --> 00:06:59,368
[SPEAKER_01]: It happened on numerous occasions, obviously, but it's like...

81
00:06:59,388 --> 00:07:08,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm keeping the precepts, I'm sensory-strained, and that's all, like... And I'm meditating, like, you know, an hour in the morning, an hour in the evening, because that's the practice.

82
00:07:08,153 --> 00:07:11,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I pick that up as a duty of somebody who wants to practice.

83
00:07:11,836 --> 00:07:12,616
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got to meditate.

84
00:07:12,636 --> 00:07:16,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got to watch your belly or nostrils or whatever sensations.

85
00:07:17,459 --> 00:07:19,060
[SPEAKER_01]: I was doing it all.

86
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:21,042
[SPEAKER_01]: And the example I always give was like...

87
00:07:22,050 --> 00:07:24,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to go out with friends.

88
00:07:24,351 --> 00:07:29,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, nothing unwholesome, nothing against the priesthood, just wholesome company, fun, getting together.

89
00:07:30,694 --> 00:07:37,657
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if I just go out with friends, obviously, I have a desire for company, for people.

90
00:07:39,518 --> 00:07:40,078
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel guilty.

91
00:07:41,332 --> 00:07:46,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not against the precepts, but I knew enough to see that it's rooted in desire.

92
00:07:46,653 --> 00:07:47,453
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I felt guilty.

93
00:07:47,933 --> 00:07:49,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do now?

94
00:07:49,473 --> 00:07:51,154
[SPEAKER_01]: See, how do I deal with this?

95
00:07:51,174 --> 00:07:57,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let me do my practice duty of meditation, so that then I can go out.

96
00:07:59,235 --> 00:08:01,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then I would sit and meditate and go out.

97
00:08:02,856 --> 00:08:04,976
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I would come back and meditate.

98
00:08:05,196 --> 00:08:05,896
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I'm fine.

99
00:08:06,197 --> 00:08:07,937
[SPEAKER_01]: See, I have offset

100
00:08:09,006 --> 00:08:10,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of desire through meditation.

101
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:12,707
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was my life for a while.

102
00:08:13,188 --> 00:08:24,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Fortunately, not too long, because things were becoming more and more apparent, in a sense that, well, actually, see, the only reason I was doing this sense of duty is because I could not stop acting out of desire.

103
00:08:25,332 --> 00:08:31,555
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I realized that it would have been better off, and it will be better off, if I don't sit and meditate out of that duty at all.

104
00:08:32,355 --> 00:08:35,379
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead, don't act out of desire in the first place.

105
00:08:35,739 --> 00:08:40,424
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if I didn't go out, I wouldn't have to be calming myself down with the meditation.

106
00:08:41,305 --> 00:08:45,010
[SPEAKER_01]: People say, well, you know, meditation is good, it calms you down.

107
00:08:45,110 --> 00:08:47,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the question is, why are you disturbed in the first place?

108
00:08:49,355 --> 00:08:52,156
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only... you can't be disturbed by circumstances.

109
00:08:52,196 --> 00:08:54,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be disturbed by the stone rolling and so on.

110
00:08:54,937 --> 00:09:00,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only be disturbed by letting Mara in, who disturbs you, through greed, aversion, delusion.

111
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:11,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody needs meditation techniques to keep themselves calm, means they're perpetually disturbing themselves, through their own acts and their own ignorance of their own intentions.

112
00:09:12,385 --> 00:09:16,027
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, if you were to contemplate that long enough, it would sink in.

113
00:09:16,647 --> 00:09:21,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would recognize, oh, important as important, and unimportant as unimportant.

114
00:09:21,789 --> 00:09:23,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's important to not disturb myself.

115
00:09:24,050 --> 00:09:26,612
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's unimportant to manage it this way or that way.

116
00:09:26,772 --> 00:09:27,652
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all secondary.

117
00:09:28,172 --> 00:09:36,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I meditated for an hour, did belly bhavana, or watched my nostrils, all of that is now secondary to the fact that I acted out of the desire first.

118
00:09:37,917 --> 00:09:40,258
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm not disturbing myself,

119
00:09:41,191 --> 00:09:54,083
[SPEAKER_01]: If I'm not giving in to the desires, if I'm able to contain the impulse that's making me act out of unwholesome, even if it's not necessarily against the precepts, then, if there is any disturbance, sure, I'll calm myself within that.

120
00:09:54,483 --> 00:10:01,750
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you realize pretty quickly, well, I am already composed, simply by not spilling out through all these acts.

121
00:10:03,081 --> 00:10:21,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, in other words, instead of sitting and mechanically trying to compensate for your lack of restraint, for your lack of understanding, compensate for that by actually sitting down for an hour in the morning, an hour in the evening, and contemplate nature of your experience.

122
00:10:21,969 --> 00:10:22,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you suffer?

123
00:10:23,610 --> 00:10:25,250
[SPEAKER_01]: How did this disturbance come to be?

124
00:10:27,031 --> 00:10:29,012
[SPEAKER_01]: All of the aspects that are relevant to that.

125
00:10:30,190 --> 00:10:32,912
[SPEAKER_01]: And do that every day, twice a day.

126
00:10:33,432 --> 00:10:41,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that, at least, can sort of... will be, actually, feeding your perspective and increasing your understanding.

127
00:10:41,999 --> 00:10:53,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, next time, when something unpleasant happens or something challenging happens, again, you won't need to be resorting to a special set of circumstances where you will stop thinking and just calm down while this problem will remain waiting for you.

128
00:10:54,048 --> 00:10:56,350
[SPEAKER_01]: You will already see through it and see the nature of it.

129
00:10:56,410 --> 00:10:59,312
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you'll be like, oh, yeah, of course, how could it be different?

130
00:10:59,792 --> 00:11:01,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Attachment leads to suffering.

131
00:11:01,193 --> 00:11:02,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is pretty clear.

132
00:11:02,053 --> 00:11:07,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why in the suttas as well, like, monks, they've been contemplating things rightly, committed to it, day in, day out.

133
00:11:07,997 --> 00:11:09,278
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, he comes back from pīṇḍapaṭṭhā.

134
00:11:09,678 --> 00:11:13,801
[SPEAKER_01]: He purifies his mind from covetousness, from greed, from aversion.

135
00:11:14,161 --> 00:11:15,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where is lust in this?

136
00:11:15,682 --> 00:11:16,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there lust in this experience?

137
00:11:16,903 --> 00:11:18,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Can lust arise in this experience?

138
00:11:18,864 --> 00:11:19,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Can ill will arise in this?

139
00:11:20,285 --> 00:11:21,565
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the basis for ill will?

140
00:11:21,686 --> 00:11:25,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, you know, conceit, anger, no wanting to deal with discomfort.

141
00:11:25,568 --> 00:11:26,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so

142
00:11:29,853 --> 00:11:33,354
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a myriad of directions you can do, if you start asking right questions.

143
00:11:34,134 --> 00:11:37,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, those monks, who are diligent like that, would go, and then, they would encounter something.

144
00:11:38,916 --> 00:11:42,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Something shocking, or something unpleasant, and it was like, they would see through it.

145
00:11:42,737 --> 00:11:52,581
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, they would have done the work beforehand, and the last drop of the truth would have been, when circumstances show them what they already understood, and then, it would just sink in.

146
00:11:52,621 --> 00:11:59,143
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh yeah, of course, life is dukkha, the way out is like what I've been doing, and the mind gets liberated there and then.

147
00:11:59,623 --> 00:12:09,391
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people, like, obviously, they don't see the background of this whole thing, which is, like, this intense development, sense restraint, guarding on the sense door, contemplation, day in, day out, purifying your mind from the unwholesome states.

148
00:12:10,211 --> 00:12:14,595
[SPEAKER_01]: You read the sutta and you only see, oh, he met robbers, they tried to kill him, he became an arahant.

149
00:12:15,215 --> 00:12:16,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he was so lucky.

150
00:12:16,076 --> 00:12:16,856
[SPEAKER_01]: I was like, no, no, no.

151
00:12:16,957 --> 00:12:23,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Those circumstances just emphasized what he already been contemplating and been understanding.

152
00:12:24,231 --> 00:12:26,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so, that's really, like, boils down to that.

153
00:12:26,613 --> 00:12:34,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you actually tame your behavior, contain it, and then contemplate, and you will have plenty of material for contemplation.

154
00:12:34,259 --> 00:12:40,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Every single desire, every attachment, everything you don't want to give up.

155
00:12:40,744 --> 00:12:41,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Loads of stuff.

156
00:12:41,965 --> 00:12:42,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Pick it up.

157
00:12:43,166 --> 00:12:43,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about it.

158
00:12:43,906 --> 00:12:45,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about its cessation.

159
00:12:45,708 --> 00:12:46,888
[SPEAKER_01]: See why you can't give it up.

160
00:12:47,869 --> 00:12:48,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Then give it up, maybe.

161
00:12:49,605 --> 00:12:52,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, scare yourself, even, through contemplation.

162
00:12:52,146 --> 00:12:55,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but, see, I don't even want to think about giving this up.

163
00:12:55,267 --> 00:12:56,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, hold on, hold on.

164
00:12:57,048 --> 00:12:57,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

165
00:12:58,309 --> 00:13:01,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Why don't I... why am I refusing to go in that direction?

166
00:13:01,270 --> 00:13:09,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's... there is no development there, if I willingly are choosing to turn a blind eye to something that's going to be quite possibly a deep attachment.

167
00:13:10,214 --> 00:13:11,995
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, yeah, just give away your attachment.

168
00:13:12,015 --> 00:13:13,275
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, you need to understand it.

169
00:13:14,636 --> 00:13:18,698
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just throw it away, because then you're just... something else will take its place.

170
00:13:19,796 --> 00:13:27,159
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand that whenever there is a phenomenon of attachment, an attitude of attachment in your experience, that equals suffering.

171
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:39,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I purify my experience from any... finding any safety in any... any dearness, any delight in external things of sense objects and so on, there is no basis for suffering anymore.

172
00:13:40,525 --> 00:13:44,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you do it, the clearer it becomes and the confidence will be born from it.

173
00:13:45,093 --> 00:13:50,776
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you won't need any more suttas to inspire you or the utterance of another how to contemplate a way to go.

174
00:13:51,296 --> 00:14:00,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much anything you encounter, you'll be seeing the principles in it, questioning if they're unclear, for that right reason, for the right purpose.

175
00:14:03,863 --> 00:14:12,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you take, you know, you can sort of start by taking for granted, okay, my idea of attachment, my idea of suffering, and now I need to kind of find out and figure out the connection between the two.

176
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:26,826
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the correct contemplation, as I understand it anyway, would be more on the level of, like, wait, if I were to even kind of fully understand the first word in this sentence, attachment, then I would...

177
00:14:27,026 --> 00:14:29,027
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd be free from attachment and free from self.

178
00:14:29,227 --> 00:14:30,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's, that's exactly.

179
00:14:30,367 --> 00:14:36,350
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you would, probably, everybody would start by these kind of not so accurate contemplations.

180
00:14:36,890 --> 00:14:41,752
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, if you keep going in that direction, you were like, hold on, but do I even know what attachment is?

181
00:14:41,772 --> 00:14:43,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Just saying attachment, what is it?

182
00:14:44,723 --> 00:14:45,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I convey it to somebody else?

183
00:14:46,044 --> 00:14:47,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I describe it?

184
00:14:47,544 --> 00:14:50,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would I need to use some anecdotal examples?

185
00:14:50,846 --> 00:14:54,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I describe the principle behind it, the universal nature of attachment?

186
00:14:54,187 --> 00:14:54,647
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

187
00:14:55,888 --> 00:14:57,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have attachment without desire?

188
00:14:59,069 --> 00:15:00,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have attachment without craving?

189
00:15:03,231 --> 00:15:10,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, can you have attachment without overlooking the basic nature of your own experience, of your own body, of your own senses?

190
00:15:10,795 --> 00:15:11,455
[SPEAKER_01]: I was like, oh, no.

191
00:15:12,536 --> 00:15:17,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will, like, the more you do it, and that's what I mean, like, you need to start doing it.

192
00:15:17,801 --> 00:15:25,408
[SPEAKER_01]: These contemplations will not, like, magically just take you all the way to asking the right questions and becoming an arahant through clarity and so on.

193
00:15:26,569 --> 00:15:29,091
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be inaccurate in the beginning, but the more you do it,

194
00:15:29,853 --> 00:15:42,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the more precise your own questioning will become, to the point where you don't even have to ask the question, just the way you look at things in your experience, it's already... questions are already implicit, the right direction is already implicit.

195
00:15:44,178 --> 00:15:55,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like through self-questioning, on the basis of guarding of the sense doors and precepts and watching your intentions, you are narrowing down to where the actual problem is.

196
00:15:57,684 --> 00:16:17,873
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, like the contemplation is something... the attitude of contemplation is something that could certainly take you from the state of a completely uninstructed puthujjana to a sotāpanna who now knows where the problem is and knows how to uproot it.

